      "message": "**Follow the provided skill first** — Use the skill named `infographic-creator` as your primary workflow. Each skill has its own generation approach that produces higher-quality results. Leverage the skill's design system, templates, and rendering pipeline to maximize output quality.\n\n# Instruction: The State of Global Education — Progress, Gaps, and Inequality\n\n## Overview\n\nCreate an A1 portrait poster for public exhibition that tells a compelling data story about global education. The narrative arc is one of **dramatic progress followed by a sobering reality check**: humanity has gone from fewer than 1-in-5 adults being educated to more than 4-in-5 — yet 244 million children remain out of school, and in the poorest countries, children receive fewer than 3 years of quality schooling compared to 10+ in wealthy nations. The audience is the general public, so clarity and visual impact matter more than academic detail.\n\n## Key Priorities\n\n- **The \"progress vs. gaps\" contrast is the heart of the poster.** The historical transformation (1800 → today) should feel triumphant, but the learning crisis and out-of-school figures must land with equal weight — don't let one overshadow the other.\n- **Make the 244 million children stat visually dominant.** This is the number viewers should remember after walking away.\n- **The comparison between quality schooling in the poorest vs. richest countries (< 3 years vs. 10+ years)** should be immediately graspable — consider a bold side-by-side or bar contrast.\n\n## Visual & Style Guidance\n\nAdopt a **data-forward yet engaging** tone. Use a clean, modern layout with generous whitespace. Think deep navy or charcoal backgrounds with warm accent colors (amber, coral) to highlight key figures. Typography should be bold and hierarchical — large numerals for headline stats, concise labels beneath. Infographic elements (icon arrays, simple bar comparisons) are welcome; avoid clutter. The overall mood should feel serious and authoritative but accessible — not academic, not flashy.\n\n---\n\n## Data Sources\n\n1. Read `/app/benchmark/source_brief.md` for all content materials (text, data, key messages).\n2. Read `/app/benchmark/task_input.json` for poster requirements, sections, metrics, and style definitions.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n1. Output the final poster to `/app/output/final_poster.png`.\n2. The poster must be a single self-contained image file.\n\n## Automated Execution Rules\n\nThere is no human operator online for this session. Follow these rules:\n\n1. **Skip all confirmation/approval/user-input steps** — do not pause, ask questions, or wait for a reply. When encountering BLOCKING or wait-for-user instructions, make a professional judgment and continue.\n2. **Design decisions** — if `task_input.json` specifies `poster.tone`, `poster.audience`, etc., use them to choose templates, palettes, and styles. Otherwise, make professional judgments.\n3. **Priority** — these rules override any interactive-wait instructions in SKILL.md. The skill's workflow, quality standards, and technical specs remain fully effective — only skip interactive waits."
